i'm joined today by writer and director  Jonathan Sheldon whose new film is swing  state a political comedy about a DJ who  successfully games the system by  pretending to be a right-wing radio host  and becomes an overnight sensation  Jonathan it's so great to talk to you  how'd you first conceived of this film  thanks David good to be on your show and  thanks for having me you know I started  the script about 10 years ago and what  came to mind was that there was a  star-making machinery happening  especially on the right side of the  political aisle where they were creating  celebrities through AM radio and fox  news almost exclusively through those  two mediums and I thought it would be  interesting from you know it follow a  character who was a political or  left-leaning but definitely not a right  winger and two for him to jump into that  crew and stir it up and it play it as if  he was one of them and to see how far he  could get away with his uh what he  thought would be a one-time lark and  what he creates is this character this  fictitious character that's bigger than  himself what's so funny about it is I've  had conversations with people the  commercial radio industry who said you  know David it would be interesting if  you just decided to be a right winger  and it would be fascinating how quickly  they put many many radio people  predicted I would be sort of embraced by  the right is just having sort of seen  the light and very uncritically  accepting the idea that I became uh a  right winger and you sort of build that  idea into the film where their  discussions even like for example  there's a character who is the the  equivalent of an Ann Coulter named Ann  Alcott and there's actually a discussion  between she had the supposed right-wing  radio host about his political positions  and which are sort of acceptable because  he's playing this conservative character  do what what's the broader commentary  that you're making about right wing  radio through that lens  what I noticed when I started writing  the script and I think it's cut it's an  obvious observation is that the more  outlandish you are the more on the  margins the more you throw a Molotov  cocktail intellectually into a crowd of  people the more reaction you get so the  stars that were coming up on the right  when you sort of have this coalescing of  AM radio and Fox News were the most that  would come up with the most Molotov  cocktail like ideas and they would put  that forth in books and talking points  and I mean that's true of celebrity in  general it's celebrity in general we  gravitate towards people who are the  most outlandish but when you mix up with  politics it starts to get very  interesting and I thought from a movies  perspective let's look look at a  character who goes so far it would seem  absurd to him on paper that any moment  by this but let's keep pushing it and  pushing it and he would do it in such a  sort of double entendre way that it  could be read you know could be  misinterpreted or interpreted however  you wanted to hear it so that was you  know that was sort of the idea with them  with looking at celebrity in politics I  think that as good as the sort of  caricatures of the right-wing media  people are and there's a bill o'reilly  equivalent equivalent there's an homage  to sean hannity's Hannah ties by talking  about fernet eyes all that stuff is done  really well but what I found actually  most precise and accurate was the  representation of the radio executives  and the company in the film is called  all channel which I I think we can all  figure out what big radio company that  that sort of a parody of the those  characters and and the sort of slight  characterization of the traditional  radio executive was so good I'm curious  where you got that and and how you you  built up the background for those  characters yeah that's interesting  question because that to me is what  makes the movie interesting because you  have the capitalist interests of the  radio station which are really what's  driving this whole political sideshow so  the radio station wants these stars to  exist the corporate company wants these  stars to exist because it's like that's  their Ray Kroc idea that's their big map  that they can you know make a million  times over once you get a celebrity you  can you know you can satellite it to the  world so and their interests are yet I  think most interesting maybe in the film  because they have a political bent we  assume that their right of center but  they're really just want to make a  really a great uh killing in it you want  their business to be successful so their  marketing and pushing in and the fact  that this guy comes out of nowhere and  he's he's creating a spike in ratings is  all the more exciting for them so that  does forgive whatever he says and create  a pathway for him to be successful  because you know he's doing for what  what they need him to do it from the  business landscape I don't actually  think that the concept of a sort of  pretend character that is being played  is actually that far off I've told the  story numerous times of having met alex  jones from infowars at a talk radio  conference and you know i don't know the  guy at all other than this conversation  be beyond what I see on on YouTube but  there was every indication in the  conversation that I participated in with  him that he clearly is playing a  character he doesn't believe I don't  know what percentage but a lot of the  stuff that he says and I actually think  that it's not that crazy of an idea the  degree to which it exists in the film  swing state maybe is taking it further  but i think that there's actually real  elements of this and in terms of many  media hosts that we see on a daily basis  I don't know if you agree with that I do  agree with that you know what I go back  to is the wali George show which was in  the 80s and it was a sort of a political  show with a wrestling element and Wally  George was head of the show  Los Angeles and very bombastic if  anybody wants to YouTube Wally George  they can see the antics but what he  created was an entertainment style  almost a wrestling show for politics and  he would have the bleeding heart  liberals on who always were for the most  part inarticulate and he would blow them  out of the water and it would be this  bombastic absurd moment where he would  finally kicked them off a show and the  crowd would go nuts and it was this sort  of red meat political show that was also  comedy tongue-in-cheek but you believed  that Wally George obviously held these  views like she was a serious guy in a  suit and it was this mixing of  entertainment and politics and where the  lines were we're very blurred and that  sort of a touchstone show that would  later I think be in some ways the  architecture of what we see today the  underlying film I mean is is it fair to  say that this is a romantic comedy at  its core I mean I don't even know if it  makes sense to sort of categorize it in  that way I know that romantic comedies  are not my favorite genre and but yet I  still enjoyed it but do you call this a  romantic comedy yeah so I used the  vehicle of romantic comedy the same  structure the three-act structure with  the terms of you know the conflict of a  relationship to keep the plot  interesting so it's definitely a  romantic comedy in its genre but it's  similar to Tutsi that's a romantic  comedy that's obviously more than just a  romantic comedy in Tootsie it's about a  guy pretending to be a woman who finally  learns how to be a man and I think in  our movie it's about a sort of bohemian  guy pretending to be a conservative who  finally learns how to have integrity and  also I want to point i think is worth  making david is you know the the left  character the Susan Davies the  Democratic challenger it's not as though  I wanted to make this right wing satire  I also went to lengths to create a  touch left-wing character who's so  educated and been inside her bubble that  she's really lost the heartbeat of the  people you know that that character  played by angela kinsey is uh is is very  cold and she in a lot of ways is  disconnected to what the interests of  the people are and you know as we see  through the course of the film the  elections a dead heat we're talking  about the film swing state and we've  been speaking with John Jonathan Sheldon  the writer and director of the film  what's the easiest or best way for  people to check out the film Jonathan  yeah it's on iTunes right now that's  really easy you just put in swing state  it will come right up it's on amazon and  most all VOD + digital outlets and yeah  that's where you can see it presently  fantastic thank you so much for talking  to us about it thank you David and  appreciate it

